## 4. The mechanical check — two, and the weaker one is the one you'd reach for

**(a) `%`-arity, statically.** Ruff `F507` / pyflakes' percent-format checker compares placeholder count to tuple arity. It fires here because CPython merges adjacent string literals into a **single `ast.Constant`** at parse time, so the checker sees all five placeholders against the four-element tuple. There is **no lint configuration anywhere in this repo** — a glob for `pyproject.toml`/`ruff.toml`/`.flake8`/`tox.ini`/`requirements*.txt` returned nothing — so nothing of this kind has ever run here. Cost: one command, under a second, zero LabVIEW. Better still, `c60c_astcheck.py` already walks the AST: **~20 lines added there as gate 10** gives the same result with no new dependency and no new device.

⚠️ I could not execute it (read-only). Confirm with one command before trusting it.

**(b) But (a) is the weaker answer, and I want to say so plainly.** F507 catches `%`-arity and nothing else. It would not catch a `KeyError` on `R[...]`, and **neither pyflakes nor ruff does call-signature checking** — so the `read_es(tag, target)` → `read_es(tag)` signature change that caused this would itself pass unseen at every call site. The check that matches the actual defect class is **executing the recipe with LabVIEW stubbed**: import the module with `g` replaced by a mock whose verbs return canned rows, call `main()`. Every line that does not need a real VI is exercised for free. The convention already exists (`tools/bench/selftest_*.py`, `docs/cycle27-plan.md:2731`); `tools/bench/diag_c73_import.log` shows the import-only version was run this cycle — on a *different* module, and it entered no function body.

**Ordering matters: fix the failure channel (§2) before building either gate.** A corrupted failure signal is what decides whether those gates ever get built, and by whom.

